    "available_at": "2026-11-26T15:55:57.062844+00:00",
    "body": "Ocean freight rates remain far above year-ago levels, and relief is likely to be gradual, a new analysis finds, as the market moves from “disruption management” to “transition management.”\nContainer market rates are up 138% year over year and 15% from August, according to DHL Global Forwarding’s November update, but prices have roughly stabilized at the level of the previous month as peak-season intensity fades. DHL ( DHL.DE ) expects rates to stay “well above last year’s baseline” in the coming months. Ahead of contract season, it says rates are unlikely to match 2025 levels, but they should soften as fourth quarter volumes decline amid the traditional lull.\nGlobal demand is up 5% year to date and 4% year over year; Asian exports are up 8% year to date. DHL describes growth as increasingly uneven — Asian exports and secondary trades are strong while backhauls lag. On the Far East–Europe westbound trade, demand is up 9% year to date, while the backhaul is flat at 0%.\nCapacity and congestion\nGlobal container fleet capacity is forecast to grow 4% in 2026, below the roughly 6% annual average of the past decade. More than 10% of the current fleet, or 3.6 million TEUs, is tied up in congested ports, and DHL puts the reduction in effective capacity at about 15%, largely from congestion in Asia after typhoons hit Chinese ports in July and August.\nGlobal net vessel capacity growth is forecast at 4% in 2026, 9% in 2027 and 15% in 2028. DHL says new capacity should exceed nominal trade growth as major carriers have gone on an historic order binge for newbuildings. Scrapping has so far been minimal, but the report warns that “fleet growth does not immediately translate into lower rates.” Terminals, yards and inland transport are limiting the benefit. Weather, geopolitics and hub-and-spoke network models, such as the Maersk (OTC: AMKBY )-Hapag-Lloyd ( HLAG.DE ) Gemini partnership, add to the strain, the analysis states.\nSuez return and reliability\nAfter three years of diverted services, more than 60% of Asia–Europe services are again routed via the Suez Canal-Red Sea. Gemini and Mediterranean Shipping Co. are returning fastest, and DHL says carriers in some cases have changed routings in transit without prior announcement. It advises shippers to confirm that cargo insurance covers Red Sea transits, war risks, general average and rerouting. The mix of Suez and diversions around Africa is causing vessel bunching on the Asia–Mediterranean lane, and DHL expects the effect to reach Asia–North Europe in December.\nAt the same time, schedule reliability has fallen: In key Asian ports, fewer than one in three vessels arrived on time. Clearing the backlog is expected to last through early summer 2027, and DHL also flags possible labor action in Europe.\nFuel costs\nVery Low Sulfur Fuel Oil (VLSFO) bunker prices are 69% above the February level and near the 2026 peak, driven by Middle East disruption and uncertainty around the Strait of Hormuz. Diesel is up as much as 75% since December 2025, so inland haulage surcharges are expected to remain. The report says shippers can return to a quarterly Bunker Adjustment Factor (BAF), freeze the Q2 BAF with Emergency Bunker Surcharges, or move to monthly bunker updates, which carry no emergency surcharges.\nThe report was released prior to the Trump administration’s new round of proposed tariffs.\nAdvice for shippers\nDHL recommends that shippers:\nUse alternative regional gateways\nAccept bookings on a different carrier, even at extra cost\nAdd buffer time and contingency plans\nBuild alternatives into rate cards\nImporters, exporters, forwarders, and Beneficial Cargo Owners should match their contract approach to each shipment’s needs, DHL recommends, with committed capacity for critical freight, hybrid models for flexible volume, and spot access for unplanned surges.\nWhy It Matters: Rate agreements don’t guarantee space or schedule reliability, and DHL expects the disruption to push shippers’ focus from price toward resilience. With rates more than double last year’s, bunker costs near their peak, and congestion lasting into mid-2027, procurement teams face a costly RFQ season.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\nAsia-US West Coast container spot rates fall below $8,000 as US avoids LA-Long Beach port strike\nLargest US-based port terminal operator appoints Maersk veteran as CEO\nSavannah handles 565,366 TEUs in October, up 25% year-over-year on strong import demand\nAsia–US East Coast ocean freight rates top $10,000, up 300% from a year ago\nTentative union deal averts strike at Port of LA-Long Beach",
